# DEFERRED-GAP FIXTURE (#[ignore]'d — the self-host renders every ITEM-level
# diagnostic at line 1, column 1, because no `Item` variant in its AST carries a
# span; see todo/t0928).
#
# The VERDICT is right on both lanes — `directive scheduler=bogus` is refused —
# so this is not a lane divergence. What differs is WHERE the compiler says the
# problem is. Rust gg points at the directive (`Directive.span`, merged in
# `src/parser/mod.rs::parse_directive`). The self-host points at line 1, which in
# this file is a comment, because `validate_directives` has only
# `IDirective(String, String)` to work with and passes `Span(0, 0)`.
#
# MEASURED at HEAD on the self-host lowerer driver:
#   error[E_UnknownDirective]: unknown directive `scheduler=bogus`
#     ┌─ …/sh_directive_diagnostic_points_at_line_one.gg:1:1
#   1 │ # DEFERRED-GAP FIXTURE (#[ignore]'d — the self-host renders every ITEM-level
#     │ ^
#
# The blank padding below is deliberate and load-bearing: it puts the directive
# well away from line 1, so a diagnostic that lands on line 1 cannot be mistaken
# for a correct one. Do not "tidy" it.
#
# The INTENDED state is the caret on the `directive` line itself — which is what
# the `#[ignore]`d `sh_directive_diagnostic_points_at_the_directive` asserts, and
# why it is RED at HEAD. Un-ignore it and promote this fixture out of
# `known_gaps/` the round the self-host AST carries item spans.



directive scheduler=bogus

void main():
    print("REACHED — the directive was not validated")
